Feature: The page that explains the encryption

  A woman leaving a tracker that sold her cycle does not want another promise. She wants to see
  what happens to one day of her life after she writes it down. The header of every page of this
  site offers her Privacy, the band at the foot offers her "How Emi protects your data", and both
  of them have taken her to a page that is not there.

  So the page is built, from the board the operator drew. It says four things, and it says them in
  this order on purpose. What happens to one day, in three numbered steps, so the order is the
  point. Who holds the keys, on a dark band, because that is the answer she came for, and the
  answer is her phone, her recovery code, and not Emi. What Emi does not defend against, in four
  cards the same size as the three above them, because a limit drawn smaller than a benefit is a
  limit that was hidden. And two ways to check all of it: the code, which is public, and the full
  document.

  Two of those four cards describe something that is designed and not built, so each one says so on
  its own face. A page about trust that lets a reader believe the sync is running has spent the
  trust it was written to earn.

  The page is built by hand from the board, in another language, through another framework.
  Nothing in that process keeps the two the same, so a browser holds the page against the board at
  three widths. What a picture cannot see is read here instead: that the words arrive in all three
  languages, that the two presses go where the board sends them, that the page names its own
  address rather than the home page, and that a crawler is told the page exists.

  Scenario: the privacy page matches the approved design pixel for pixel
    Given a woman follows Privacy from the band at the top of myemi.app
    Then she reads the four parts of the board, in its order and on its grounds
    And every sentence of the board is on the page, in English, in Russian and in Spanish
    And the headings are set in the face the board sets them in, which the site serves itself
    And the two cards for what is not built yet say so, and the one that is built says that
    And the press to the code opens the public repository, and the one beside it opens the full document
    And the page names its own address, so a search engine keeps it apart from the home page
    And the list a crawler reads names the page in each of the three languages
    And the page writes no style of its own, so her browser refuses nothing
    And a browser holds the page against the board at all three widths, and keeps the picture of any difference
